# VODNOX telephony provisioning agent — client allow-list.
#
# Install as /etc/nginx/vodnox-allow.conf. It is included by
# nginx/vodnox-provisioning.conf INSIDE the server block, immediately before a
# "deny all;" — so if this file contains no "allow" line, the provisioning API
# is closed to everyone. That is the safe default: it fails closed.
#
# What to put here
# ----------------
# The exact egress address of the host that runs the Laravel application
# (vodnox.com) — not a guessed subnet. The application host is hosted
# separately and may egress through a NAT address that is not on any internal
# range, so this value must come from the requirements check (README §11):
#
#     curl -s https://ifconfig.me        # run on the Laravel host
#     # or, if it egresses through a NAT pool, ask for the whole published pool.
#
# Then uncomment and set it, one line per address:
#
#     allow 203.0.113.10;
#     allow 203.0.113.11;
#
# Do NOT use "allow all;", "0.0.0.0/0" or a wide subnet such as /16: this API
# writes Asterisk configuration and can create or delete SIP accounts. The
# signature (HMAC-SHA256) and HTTPS remain the primary controls; this list is
# defence in depth, so keep it as narrow as the Laravel host's egress allows.
#
# IPv6, when the application host reaches the server over IPv6, is a separate
# line:
#
#     allow 2001:db8::10;
